---
// Reference / CLI / update and uninstall.
//
// Written rather than generated: the binary's own surface. Paired because
// they are the two commands that change the installation itself, and someone
// reaching for one should see what the other does.
//
// Only `update --check` is exercised by verify-transcripts. A build must never
// install or remove anything.
import DocsLayout from '../../../layouts/DocsLayout.astro';
import CliBlock from '../../../components/docs/CliBlock.astro';
---

<DocsLayout
  editPath="src/pages/docs/reference/update-and-uninstall.astro"
  title="update and uninstall"
  description="Move the binary to another release, pin it, roll it back, or remove the installation."
  section="Reference"
>
  <h1>update and uninstall</h1>
  <p>
    These two change the installation rather than a database. Everything else in the CLI leaves the
    binary alone.
  </p>

  <h2>update</h2>
  <p>Replace the binary with the latest release:</p>
  <CliBlock>strata update</CliBlock>
  <p>Ask first, without installing anything:</p>
  <CliBlock>strata update --check</CliBlock>
  <CliBlock variant="output">strata is up to date (1.2.4)</CliBlock>
  <p>
    <code>--check</code> is the form to use anywhere that should not change underneath you: a CI job,
    a container build, a shell prompt. It reports and exits.
  </p>

  <h3>Pinning and rolling back</h3>
  <p>
    <code>--version</code> installs a specific release instead of the latest, which is how you roll back
    a bad upgrade and how you hold a machine at a known version:
  </p>
  <CliBlock>strata update --version 1.2.2</CliBlock>
  <p>
    It moves in either direction. There is no separate downgrade command, and going backwards is not
    treated as unusual.
  </p>
  <p>
    A database written by a newer engine is the thing to think about before rolling back, rather
    than the binary swap itself. <a href="/docs/reference/compatibility"
      >Compatibility and versioning</a
    > covers what is safe.
  </p>

  <h2>uninstall</h2>
  <p>Remove the Strata installation from the machine:</p>
  <CliBlock>strata uninstall</CliBlock>
  <p>
    It prompts before doing anything. <code>--yes</code> skips the prompt, which is what an automated
    teardown wants and what you should think twice about typing by hand.
  </p>
  <CliBlock>strata uninstall --yes</CliBlock>

  <h3>What it does not remove</h3>
  <p>
    Your databases. They are ordinary paths you chose, and nothing here goes looking for them, so
    uninstalling does not put data at risk. Removing a database is deleting the directory yourself.
  </p>
  <p>
    Use <a href="/docs/reference/doctor">doctor</a> before uninstalling if you want to see what the installation
    actually consists of, including the home directory and the resolved configuration path.
  </p>
</DocsLayout>
